WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.425
Now you have to get back to him.

2
00:00:02.675 --> 00:00:08.575
There are several recovery turns, and the one the exam asks about is the Williamson turn.

3
00:00:08.825 --> 00:00:10.650
Look at the diagram.

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:19.000
Its whole purpose is to bring you back down your own track, so it’s the right turn at night, or any time you’ve lost sight of the person.

5
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.025
Here’s how it goes.

6
00:00:21.275 --> 00:00:25.050
One: rudder hard over, toward the side he fell from.

7
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:31.675
Two: when you’re about sixty degrees off your original course, shift the rudder hard over the other way.

8
00:00:31.925 --> 00:00:35.725
Three: steady up on the reciprocal of your original course.

9
00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:39.125
Now you’re running back down your own wake, toward him.

10
00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:42.075
So when wouldn’t you use it?

11
00:00:42.325 --> 00:00:44.525
When he’s in sight and close.

12
00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:49.625
Then a quicker single turn, like the Anderson turn, gets you back faster.

13
00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:52.250
Williamson when you’ve lost him.

14
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.950
A simple turn when you can see him.
